265
00:10:21.835 --> 00:10:23.820
Right, and just for the listener out there,

266
00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.100
UCLA was a dynasty in swimming,

267
00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.410
in the seventies and the eighties,

268
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.414
and just produced champion after champion

269
00:10:30.414 --> 00:10:33.063
and NC two A championship team one after another.

270
00:10:35.052 --> 00:10:38.120
And it was kind of Title IX

271
00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.300
that was the death knell for the program, right.

272
00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.547
Which was the law that was passed

273
00:10:42.547 --> 00:10:43.680
that said you had to have equal funding

274
00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.520
for women's sports and men's sports.

275
00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.040
And so colleges across the country had to start cutting

276
00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.170
men's programs or funding more women's programs.

277
00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.820
And UCLA ended up cutting its swimming program,

278
00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.070
which was devastating to swimming

279
00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.590
and still to this day is unbelievable to me.

280
00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:00.423
And it's unbelievable

281
00:11:00.423 --> 00:11:02.730
that they have not resuscitated the program.

282
00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.070
We're trying to work on that right now.

283
00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.540
The alumni were really working hard.

284
00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.540
UCLA just built a beautiful new 50-meter pool

285
00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:13.390
specifically for the competitive aquatics.

286
00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:18.140
So for the Water Polo team and the women's swim,

287
00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:19.080
so, we're trying to really

288
00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:20.515
get in there.
I've seen it,

289
00:11:20.515 --> 00:11:21.348
it's incredible.

290
00:11:21.348 --> 00:11:22.330
And I'm like, there's no men's swim team

291
00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:24.420
to use this facility.

292
00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.510
It's unbelievable in Southern California.

293
00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:27.900
Yeah, it's crazy.

294
00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.900
But because of that, I decided to start training at USC.

295
00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:37.680
And I had no money and pretty much living

296
00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.285
with there were about six guys in an apartment.

297
00:11:40.285 --> 00:11:42.940
I started substitute teaching and training and that's it.

298
00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.750
But in that moment, Rich and Julie,

299
00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.120
I knew that I was in my sweet spot.

300
00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.280
I knew that I was supposed to be there.

301
00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.260
I couldn't explain it to other people,

302
00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:53.860
but I knew that I was supposed to be there.

303
00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.430
And all I knew was to get up every morning

304
00:11:56.430 --> 00:12:00.120
and train and to make a long story short,

305
00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.080
I ran into a friend of mine who was a swimmer,

306
00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:04.007
And then she said, "You know what?

307
00:12:04.007 --> 00:12:06.227
"You need to contact Jonty Skinner,

308
00:12:06.227 --> 00:12:09.607
"because the U.S National team is creating this new

309
00:12:09.607 --> 00:12:13.107
"unprecedented program at the Olympic Training Center,

310
00:12:13.107 --> 00:12:14.287
"the resident national team,

311
00:12:14.287 --> 00:12:18.309
"and they said people who want to,

312
00:12:18.309 --> 00:12:19.737
"the top people, the top swimmers in the world

313
00:12:19.737 --> 00:12:22.597
"are invited to train at the Olympic Training Center.

314
00:12:22.597 --> 00:12:23.810
"You should apply."

315
00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.250
Now, I thought that this was gonna be crazy,

316
00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.180
because again, here I had never been proven

317
00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.570
in the 100-meter butterfly,

318
00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:32.940
so long course was not a strong point for me.

319
00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.460
Yeah, But you were a name in the sport.

320
00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.970
I mean, it wasn't like you were a nobody,

321
00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:38.552
everybody knew who you were.

322
00:12:38.552 --> 00:12:40.552
Oh, yeah, but again, I had no results.

323
00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.160
All my results were yards and in college,

324
00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.620
so I'm training, I'm swimming.

325
00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.960
I write Jonty Skinner a letter.

326
00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:51.110
I don't even qualify,

327
00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:54.150
my times aren't even fast enough to be a candidate.

328
00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.860
And this is where again, we can get into this,

329
00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:59.300
but this is where I really believe providence moves

330
00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.363
when we follow our passion, even if we can't explain it.

331
00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.270
So I write Jonty Skinner,

332
00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:07.770
the resident national team coach at the time, a letter.

333
00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:08.610
It was September.

334
00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.270
I remember it was September 10th.

335
00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:14.470
And I wrote him a letter and just shared it with him,

336
00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.940
just listed out an argument.

337
00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.660
This is where the whole political science and law came in.

338
00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.100
I constructed a great argument

339
00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.170
as to why I should be a good candidate.

340
00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:23.180
With a highlighter.

341
00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.363
With a highlighter, exactly.

342
00:13:25.363 --> 00:13:28.120
And I wrote him a letter, I sent it off.

343
00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.430
September 28th, I get a call from Jonty Skinner.

344
00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:35.040
And literally, I mean, Jonty is one of those guys,

345
00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:36.030
really laid back,

346
00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.290
doesn't speak a lot, doesn't say a lot,

347
00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:41.100
says what he means, but pretty monotone.

348
00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.380
So you don't know whether-

349
00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:43.213
He's a South African guy, right.

350
00:13:43.213 --> 00:13:44.120
Yeah, South African guy.

351
00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:45.710
He was a great sprinter at the time.

352
00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.060
He missed going to the Olympics

353
00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.580
because being part of the whole apartheid thing

354
00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.770
was the whole issue.
I remember that.

355
00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.340
So he really missed his thing,

356
00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.350
but became a very accomplished coach

357
00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:57.600
at University of Alabama,

358
00:13:59.519 --> 00:14:02.530
at this time they picked him to be the national team coach

359
00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:04.430
for the resident program.

360
00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:07.330
Wrote him the letter, he calls me September 28th,

361
00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.047
and he says, "Byron, just in a nutshell,

362
00:14:09.047 --> 00:14:11.147
"And it's classic.

363
00:14:11.147 --> 00:14:14.157
"I'm not giving you any emotion kinda voice.

364
00:14:14.157 --> 00:14:18.407
"If you are honest about what you put in that letter,

365
00:14:18.407 --> 00:14:21.569
"if you really think you can do

366
00:14:21.569 --> 00:14:22.567
"what you say you think you can do,

367
00:14:22.567 --> 00:14:24.467
"and you're willing to be coachable,

368
00:14:25.367 --> 00:14:27.760
"then I'll see you out here, October 3rd.

369
00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.161
Oh, nice.

370
00:14:29.161 --> 00:14:30.697
So that was.
He's got goosebumps.

371
00:14:30.697 --> 00:14:32.610
Oh, and then he hangs up the phone.

372
00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.350
I don't even remember him saying goodbye.

373
00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.967
It was like click, that was it.

374
00:14:36.967 --> 00:14:39.400
And I'm like, okay, did this just happen?

375
00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:40.470
Is this is real?

376
00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:41.810
And so I was like, "All right."

377
00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:45.010
So I ended up giving pretty much 90% of everything

378
00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:47.620
that I owned away, got out of my lease.

379
00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:48.520
All in one day.

380
00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:50.690
Got out of my lease, packed everything else

381
00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:54.240
that I had in my 1984 Audi 5000

382
00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.230
that I bought for 1400 bucks.

383
00:14:56.230 --> 00:15:00.060
And literally, drove out in $50 in my pocket

384
00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.390
and drove out to Colorado Springs,

385
00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:06.030
not knowing what was gonna meet me there.

386
00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.630
And then in 14 months,

387
00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:09.990
training at the Olympic Training Center

388
00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:11.790
after a two year layoff,

389
00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.130
went from never being world-ranked

390
00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:14.710
in a hundred-meter butterfly to being ranked

391
00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:15.800
in the top 10 in the world.

392
00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.550
And then coming within three-tenths of a second

393
00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:18.980
of making the Olympic team.

394
00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:22.310
And so that story, (clears throat) I think is the power.

395
00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:25.020
So although I didn't make the Olympic team,

396
00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.240
what I draw from that,

397
00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.990
that experience was, you know what?

398
00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:31.950
Yeah, I came up a little short,

399
00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:33.520
but failure is never final,

400
00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:34.960
especially if you know how to use it

401
00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:36.850
to fertilize your future success.

402
00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:39.600
And so it took me a while to get that.

403
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.500
But that's what I learned because leading up to that point,

404
00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:45.170
here are all the small wins.

405
00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:50.050
There's a new study out in positive psychology

406
00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:51.570
over the last 10 years.

407
00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:56.570
They stopped studying all the illness in people's heads.

408
00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:00.283
And so they're starting to study,

409
00:16:01.324 --> 00:16:02.890
well, great people who go through tough times.

410
00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:04.946
What makes 'em special?

411
00:16:04.946 --> 00:16:05.860
Well, in positive psychology,

412
00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:08.260
there's the science of small wins,

413
00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:11.680
that's the phrase that they use, the science of small wins,

414
00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:16.680
which is people who are able to truly look at and own

415
00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.563
the small wins in their life,

416
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.810
what happens is you start to create a momentum.

417
00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:25.610
Absolutely.
Even if you're going

418
00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.140
through tough times and struggles and disappointments

419
00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.440
during the times where you're knocked back or knocked down,

420
00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.210
that momentum still moves you forward.

421
00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:37.577
So even though you fall, you fall forward.

422
00:16:37.577 --> 00:16:38.410
It's like failing up.

423
00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:39.610
Exactly.
You've had

424
00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:40.760
10 little victories.

425
00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:43.663
And so then you have one setback, you can manage that.

426
00:16:43.663 --> 00:16:45.060
It's not devastating you.

427
00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.490
Yeah, exactly.
Yeah, and the lesson

428
00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.600
is that you broke a paradigm

429
00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.600
or broke someone else's belief system

430
00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:52.700
about what could be done in your own,

431
00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:54.910
pushed those boundaries and went for it.

432
00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:59.910
And you're actually a champion many times along that path.

433
00:17:01.421 --> 00:17:02.310
Yeah, definitely.

434
00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.850
And also I think implicit in that is this idea

435
00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.130
that you had an intuitive thought

436
00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:13.130
or an emotional kind of drive that might have defied logic

437
00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:17.123
or was inexplicable, like your swimming career was over

438
00:17:17.123 --> 00:17:18.000
and it was time to get on with life

439
00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.470
and you have this nagging feeling.

440
00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:22.930
And I think we're kind of preconditioned in our society

441
00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:27.930
to repress those feelings and my story and my book

442
00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:30.260
and your story is about kind of saying,

443
00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:31.830
no, I'm actually gonna listen to that.

444
00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:33.280
And I don't know where it's gonna lead me.

445
00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:36.220
And it might be crazy and people are gonna tell me,

446
00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:37.530
this is the wrong thing to do,

447
00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:39.320
but you have that something inside you,

448
00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:42.390
that's like pushing you and making that choice

449
00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:45.100
to listen to that and follow those breadcrumbs

450
00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:46.230
wherever they may lead,

451
00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.253
I think is really powerful.

452
00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:51.930
Definitely, and here's the thing

453
00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.550
I don't wanna harp on what society teaches us,

454
00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:56.463
but we've gotta be honest.

455
00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.773
We struggle with in our society,

456
00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:04.863
what I call the gravity of control and comfort,

457
00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:07.770
there is this gravity that continues

458
00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.880
to pull us into complacency.

459
00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.430
It keeps us safe.

460
00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:16.430
And when we're safe, all it does, it keeps us perpetuating

461
00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.804
and moving down the status quo.

462
00:18:19.804 --> 00:18:21.080
And if the status quo, isn't your passion,

463
00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:24.060
isn't something that you're really called to do,

464
00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.520
you're doing it for logical reasons

465
00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.970
but reasons that don't resonate with your heart,

466
00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.350
you start living in this state of quiet desperation

467
00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.593
and you start becoming a drone.

468
00:18:34.728 --> 00:18:36.100
And here's the crazy part.

469
00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.280
People get really good at becoming drones.

470
00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.790
They become very competent and skilled in their profession.

471
00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:44.690
And it then keeps them even more stuck,

472
00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:46.840
because they get a family.

473
00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:50.070
We all go through this, get a family, you have a house,

474
00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:51.620
all these different things you now use

475
00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.320
as logical responsible excuses,

476
00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.603
not to truly pursue your passion.

477
00:18:57.802 --> 00:18:58.930
And one of the things

478
00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.590
that although I didn't learn this lesson,

479
00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.700
after the race, it was years later

480
00:19:05.704 --> 00:19:07.690
that I look back on that race,

481
00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:10.030
that the lessons really started to merge for me.

482
00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:14.490
But during that time, after 96,

483
00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:17.163
I floated for a few weeks.

484
00:19:18.142 --> 00:19:19.553
And I just floated, in fact,

485
00:19:21.472 --> 00:19:24.270
I went gotta Dodge, my girlfriend who's now my wife,

486
00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:26.500
my girlfriend and fiance at the time

487
00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:28.730
was playing overseas in Greece.

488
00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:32.270
She was playing pro indoor volleyball in Greece.

489
00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:35.040
And so I decided to just, you know what?

490
00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.963
I'm gonna head on over to Greece.

491
00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:42.444
So I convinced my mom to loan me some money.

492
00:19:42.444 --> 00:19:44.680
And I literally hung out in Greece

493
00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:46.330
for two or three weeks.

494
00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:47.900
Yeah, that's cool.

495
00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:52.900
Well, I think that we're also sort of culturally compelled

496
00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.840
to sort of make a decision immediately,

497
00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:59.320
like, okay, that chapter ended, what are you doing now?

498
00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.760
You need to know immediately and we don't take enough time

499
00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.290
to kind of breathe and catch our breath

500
00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:05.123
and go, you know what?

501
00:20:05.123 --> 00:20:08.260
It's okay if I just not make a decision right now

502
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.920
for a little bit and collect myself

503
00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:12.200
and figure out what's next.

504
00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.540
I think ultimately it'll set you on a better trajectory

505
00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:17.950
if you allow yourself that space, as opposed to,

506
00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.110
oh, my God, this is over.

507
00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:20.610
I need to get a job right away

508
00:20:21.535 --> 00:20:24.150
or go into some kind of tailspin panic over it.

509
00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:29.070
Well, and I wish I could say, Rich, that I knew that.

510
00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:33.190
And I had the courage at that moment to pursue my passion.

511
00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:37.733
My next phase, my next chapter after I got back from Greece,

512
00:20:38.857 --> 00:20:40.627
I was like, "I gotta get a job.

513
00:20:40.627 --> 00:20:41.827
"I gotta get a job."

514
00:20:42.882 --> 00:20:47.630
And so a position opened up to be an assistant coach

515
00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:50.020
at UCLA, but for the women's team

516
00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:52.000
and the head coach at the time

517
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.158
approached me, Cindy Gallagher.

518
00:20:54.158 --> 00:20:56.088
And she said, "I'd love for you to come

519
00:20:56.088 --> 00:20:57.170
"and help me with my sprinters."

520
00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.310
And it was a job and it was in something

521
00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.170
that I was familiar with, so I ended up taking it.

522
00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:05.600
I gotta tell you five months into the job.

523
00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:08.410
I realized every day I step foot on the pool deck,

524
00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:09.810
I wanted to be in the water.

525
00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.130
And I was fighting this thing because it was like,

526
00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:16.350
no, your time is done, you're finished.

527
00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.090
But I wanted to be in the pool.

528
00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:19.280
And what I didn't realize

529
00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:23.010
then is I didn't necessarily want to try to reclaim

530
00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:24.170
some glory in the water.

531
00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:29.170
But again, that passion for adventure for expressing myself

532
00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.610
kinesthetically for the creative process

533
00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:37.070
that you go through when you're preparing for your event.

534
00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:40.173
All of those things were still fresh inside of me,

535
00:21:41.036 --> 00:21:43.290
yet, I was in a position where my job description

536
00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:48.170
was really clear, and I couldn't move too much

537
00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:49.420
to the left or to the right of that.

538
00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:51.630
I had to stay in that box.

539
00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.520
But yet this spirit of creativity and adventure

540
00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:56.650
and wanting to express and use my body

541
00:21:56.650 --> 00:22:00.000
and my mind creatively to produce something, an idea,

542
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.670
that was what was alive in me.

543
00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:02.923
So I struggled.

544
00:22:03.946 --> 00:22:06.330
And pretty soon after a year, I quit,

545
00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.249
I wasn't a very good assistant coach.

546
00:22:08.249 --> 00:22:09.990
(Byron and Rich laugh out loud)

547
00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.920
And I wasn't doing my job well,

548
00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.290
Cindy would've fired me anyway.

549
00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:17.565
So I ended up quitting

550
00:22:17.565 --> 00:22:19.350
and I got back in the water and started training.

551
00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:20.870
What year is this now?

552
00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.930
This is now 1998, 1998.

553
00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:26.060
So, here's the thing,

554
00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.150
so now I'm married, my wife and I are married.

555
00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:32.763
She decides that she wants to play pro beach volleyball.

556
00:22:34.097 --> 00:22:36.320
And she partnered with a person

557
00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.370
who was also new to the game.

558
00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:41.100
And at this point in 1998,

559
00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:43.640
we're married and we live in an apartment

560
00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.610
and we decide that both of us were gonna go

561
00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:48.410
for the Olympics again.

562
00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.363
And so we go for the Olympics.

563
00:22:51.432 --> 00:22:52.265
That's great.
I start training

564
00:22:52.265 --> 00:22:53.108
and swimming.

565
00:22:53.108 --> 00:22:53.941
I go back to teaching again.

566
00:22:53.941 --> 00:22:55.090
So, this is like, comeback number two.

567
00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:56.750
Comeback number two.

568
00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:59.320
Long story short, I didn't make the 2000 Olympic Games.

569
00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:00.240
My wife did.

570
00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:01.570
Did you make the trials that year?

571
00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:04.411
Yeah, I made the trials, I made the trails.

572
00:23:04.411 --> 00:23:07.390
And I injured my shoulder a couple of weeks before

573
00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:09.333
and I got psyched out.

574
00:23:09.333 --> 00:23:11.340
I mean, that's a whole 'nother story,

575
00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:14.090
but I ended up not making the Olympic team,

576
00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:16.100
but here is the beautiful thing about that moment,

577
00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:20.240
in 1998 again, one of those kernels of confirmation

578
00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:22.460
that's like, you're on the right path.

579
00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:24.220
Stay the course.

580
00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:27.670
So in 98, my wife and I decide

581
00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:29.960
that we're gonna pursue the Olympics,

582
00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:31.210
but we gonna do it full-on.

583
00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:32.560
So we couldn't really work.

584
00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:35.840
So, I quit my job as a substitute teacher,

585
00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.490
'cause again, that's what I knew.

586
00:23:38.752 --> 00:23:42.530
I wasn't a coach, said, I can't substitute teach.

587
00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:44.650
I need to focus on swimming.

588
00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.507
Annette says, "I need to focus on pro beach volleyball."

589
00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:52.130
Incidentally, she ended up making the Olympic team in 2000.

590
00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:54.730
So we got one Olympian in family, so that's awesome.

591
00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:57.580
But here's again,

592
00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:59.930
here's where that providence comes in.

593
00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:02.610
No later, maybe it was maybe a month later,

594
00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:04.630
we're both into this.

595
00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:08.770
We don't have money and we're wondering what's gonna happen?

596
00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:12.990
I get a call from a casting agent in LA

597
00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.747
and they said, "You know what?"

598
00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:17.623
I even forgot,

599
00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:22.620
I forget what company this was, what gaming company,

600
00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:25.570
but they were creating a game

601
00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:28.388
that was revolving around the Olympics,

602
00:24:28.388 --> 00:24:30.460
and the swimming portion was Mark Spitz,

603
00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:32.180
that was the character they were using.

604
00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:34.700
And they wanted to do a commercial with Mark Spitz

605
00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:39.560
and then a backdrop or other swimmers.

606
00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.640
So I was an extra in this commercial.

607
00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:45.890
So I get this call, I'm an extra,

608
00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:47.158
I gotta tell you.

609
00:24:47.158 --> 00:24:47.991
They're like we need a black guy.

610
00:24:47.991 --> 00:24:49.161
We need a black guy.

611
00:24:49.161 --> 00:24:50.781
His name is Byron, he's the only one.

612
00:24:50.781 --> 00:24:51.953
(all laugh out loud)

613
00:24:51.953 --> 00:24:54.290
Exactly, we need a black swimmer.

614
00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:55.123
Who can we find?

615
00:24:55.123 --> 00:24:56.300
Yeah, there's only one.

616
00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.133
Yeah, exactly.

617
00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:02.360
So we go, and literally, I spend the whole day

618
00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.440
and all we're doing are 25s

619
00:25:05.399 --> 00:25:06.290
and I happen to be in the lane

620
00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:07.890
right next to Mark Spitz, right.

621
00:25:08.917 --> 00:25:09.750
And so we're doing this back-

622
00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:13.104
By the way, Mark continues to swim masters at UCLA.

623
00:25:13.104 --> 00:25:14.450
And if you're a master swimmer in Southern California,

624
00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:16.530
you can be on the UCLA masters team

625
00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:18.393
and he still shows up and swims.

626
00:25:20.483 --> 00:25:23.010
And side note, Mark Spitz is hilarious

627
00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:26.624
and he doesn't mean to be that's why he's so funny to me,

628
00:25:26.624 --> 00:25:29.297
because he'll be talking, we'll be swimming and all,

629
00:25:29.297 --> 00:25:31.220
and all of a sudden, he'll tell a story

630
00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:34.950
of how he broke the world record in warmup.

631
00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:36.220
(Julie and Rich laugh out loud)

632
00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:37.600
And he's not boasting.

633
00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.690
He's a caricature of himself.

634
00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.860
He's almost like a cartoon version of his own personality.

635
00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:45.180
He is, and everything is true.

636
00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.490
And you're like, this guy is just a dynamo,

637
00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:51.910
but all of his over-the-top stories are actually true.

638
00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:55.440
He did break the Olympic record in the 200 free

639
00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:56.793
as he was warming up.

640
00:25:57.663 --> 00:25:58.496
It was crazy.

641
00:25:58.496 --> 00:25:59.329
And what year was that?

642
00:25:59.329 --> 00:26:00.170
It was his comeback.

643
00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:01.070
Remember that whole thing.

644
00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:01.950
Yeah, his comeback

645
00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:04.150
is for 92.
It was around the same time.

646
00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:05.647
Oh, it was earlier.
It's from 1992.

647
00:26:05.647 --> 00:26:07.910
So, in fact, we knew each other

648
00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:09.770
'cause he started training with UCLA

649
00:26:10.612 --> 00:26:12.130
and he swam in the sprint lane

650
00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:17.110
from 1990 all the way up to the 1992 Olympic Games,

651
00:26:17.110 --> 00:26:18.490
or at trials.

652
00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:22.233
But I went back to this commercial.

653
00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:27.310
I get the commercial, book the commercial it's done,

654
00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:30.160
to this day, I've never seen the commercial.

655
00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:35.160
Never seen it run on air, but between 1998 and 2000,

656
00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:39.220
I kept getting these nice little checks,

657
00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:42.810
residual checks in the mail, I mean, again,

658
00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:45.360
one day's work paid for my SAG card.

659
00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:46.710
So we got insurance

660
00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:50.959
and we were able to subsist for two years.

661
00:26:50.959 --> 00:26:51.940
That's awesome.
That's amazing.

662
00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.480
While we trained.

663
00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:54.440
That's amazing.
It was incredible.

664
00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:56.141
It was incredible.

665
00:26:56.141 --> 00:26:58.550
It is that crazy magical spiritual equation

666
00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:01.430
that like when you're heart is in alignment

667
00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:03.530
and you're walking purpose and faith

668
00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.060
and you don't know where it's gonna lead you,

669
00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:07.210
that you get provided for.

670
00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:08.280
I talk about that in the book

671
00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.280
and it seems weird or whatever,

672
00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:13.677
but it's been proven to me so many times in my life.

673
00:27:13.677 --> 00:27:16.726
And then I hear stories like yours all the time.

674
00:27:16.726 --> 00:27:21.007
It doesn't often look like what you think

675
00:27:21.007 --> 00:27:21.840
it's gonna look like.

676
00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:22.673
No, it's never.

677
00:27:22.673 --> 00:27:23.506
You can never predict it,

678
00:27:23.506 --> 00:27:24.339
you don't know what

679
00:27:24.339 --> 00:27:25.651
it's gonna look like.
It's gonna be very

680
00:27:25.651 --> 00:27:27.403
unconventional and very creative,

681
00:27:28.279 --> 00:27:30.530
but it always comes.

682
00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.760
And that's what's scary and it's scary,

683
00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:36.040
and if you're human, you get scared by that.

684
00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:39.540
Because again, we're conditioned for comfort and control.

685
00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.570
We gravitate toward comfort and control in our lives,

686
00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:47.570
but the creative process it's not control-

687
00:27:48.196 --> 00:27:49.350
It's open.
No, you have

688
00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.960
to surrender that control.

689
00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:54.310
You have to be willing to let go

690
00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:57.290
and be willing to receive whatever

691
00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:58.623
is going to come your way.

692
00:27:59.742 --> 00:28:00.590
And you can relate to this, Rich,

693
00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:05.590
just in training for crazy ultra stuff,

694
00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:10.023
there comes a point and we call it being in the zone,

695
00:28:11.292 --> 00:28:15.100
but you reach a point in your race

696
00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:17.710
or your event or whatever it is,

697
00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:21.020
it's a crossroads and that crossroads

698
00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:22.400
you have to make a choice,

699
00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:26.570
either I decide to keep control

700
00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:28.450
and do this through willpower,

701
00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:31.010
or you decide to let go

702
00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.710
and you decide to just go into the zone,

703
00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:36.120
you decide to say, you know what?

704
00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.090
I'm gonna chase this.

705
00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:39.933
And the pain is still there.

706
00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:43.740
The courage you need to fight through stuff is still there.

707
00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:46.000
All of that, that doesn't go away.

708
00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:50.101
But at some point, you decide to let yourself

709
00:28:50.101 --> 00:28:51.690
be totally captured in the moment,

710
00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:54.620
and then that's where breakthrough happens.

711
00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:56.335
Absolutely.

712
00:28:56.335 --> 00:28:57.935
And I talk about that a lot too.

713
00:28:59.072 --> 00:29:01.820
And the power of surrender and the beauty of surrender

714
00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:06.486
and it's a confusing concept for somebody

715
00:29:06.486 --> 00:29:07.319
who's not familiar with it

716
00:29:07.319 --> 00:29:08.650
because the immediate kind of knee jerk reaction

717
00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.890
is, well, that's giving up, like why?

718
00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:11.980
What are you talking about?

719
00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.783
I don't understand that.

720
00:29:13.783 --> 00:29:15.610
And I'm like, "That's not what it is at all."

721
00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:16.970
You still take the actions,

722
00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:18.410
you do all the necessary things,

723
00:29:18.410 --> 00:29:22.163
but you're relinquishing your attachment to the results.

724
00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:27.293
You sort of open yourself

725
00:29:27.293 --> 00:29:29.343
up to greater possibilities at times.

726
00:29:30.339 --> 00:29:31.172
And you have a great story about that.

727
00:29:31.172 --> 00:29:34.007
There's one race you told Julie and I,

728
00:29:34.007 --> 00:29:35.548
this story about that one race

729
00:29:35.548 --> 00:29:36.470
where you were struggling with that, right.

730
00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:38.000
You had the flu.

731
00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:39.283
Can you tell that story?

732
00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.093
I'll tell you two stories.

733
00:29:43.093 --> 00:29:45.160
One, I mean quickly, the whole idea,

734
00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:47.999
I mean, the race where I was just weak and I was sick

735
00:29:47.999 --> 00:29:50.800
and I had the flu, didn't wanna compete,

736
00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:53.903
didn't wanna swim, didn't think, and being a swimmer,

737
00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:57.457
you play all these head games with yourself.

738
00:29:57.457 --> 00:29:59.257
You wonder, am I gonna hit my taper?

739
00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:05.140
You train a whole year hoping that you peak

740
00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:07.190
at the right moment, only to drop

741
00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:09.510
two or three-tenths of a second off your time.

742
00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:11.395
And that was just for a year.

743
00:30:11.395 --> 00:30:13.293
It's kind of insanity when you put it that way.

744
00:30:14.346 --> 00:30:15.563
What were we doing?

745
00:30:15.563 --> 00:30:16.557
I know, it's crazy.

746
00:30:16.557 --> 00:30:18.880
It's like the payoff, the return on our investment.

747
00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:20.800
I mean, we're being gypped.

748
00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:22.530
Well, anyway, so I'm playing

749
00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:24.513
with all these head games and all,

750
00:30:25.988 --> 00:30:27.860
and I'm in the middle of the race

751
00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:29.597
and at some point, again,

752
00:30:29.597 --> 00:30:32.438
it came to that crossroad where it was, okay,

753
00:30:32.438 --> 00:30:34.350
am I just gonna give into this moment?

754
00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:39.350
Or am I gonna buy into the logical lies that I told myself?

755
00:30:40.573 --> 00:30:42.130
And the answer was no, in that moment,

756
00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:45.700
I allowed myself to give in to what I call now,

757
00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:47.163
giving into your greatness.

758
00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:51.517
We all have three people

759
00:30:51.517 --> 00:30:53.190
in fighting for a position in our lives.

760
00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:55.640
We can either be our average self,

761
00:30:55.640 --> 00:30:57.140
we could be our defeated self,

762
00:30:57.140 --> 00:30:58.840
or we could be our epic self.

763
00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:03.290
And our epic self is the way that we do that is by learning

764
00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:05.250
to give in to our own greatness.

765
00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:08.230
And we do that in these little moments of decision.

766
00:31:08.230 --> 00:31:11.740
And so in that race, I made that decision,

767
00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:15.080
it was just a shift to allow,

768
00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:17.370
again, not looking at the outcome,

769
00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.770
not trying to will myself to win,

770
00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.890
but instead recognizing that, you know what?

771
00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:24.770
I have everything that I need in this moment

772
00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:27.220
to accomplish what I'm called to do at this time.

773
00:31:28.111 --> 00:31:29.973
That was the moment.

774
00:31:32.387 --> 00:31:33.930
And the outcome was great,

775
00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:35.240
I ended up winning

776
00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.210
and getting my best time and everything.

777
00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:39.400
But again, that was just one of those small moments.

778
00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.163
And I was sick.

779
00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:46.883
All the excuses that we had leaning into to the race,

780
00:31:49.627 --> 00:31:51.149
I didn't have a great taper,

781
00:31:51.149 --> 00:31:54.080
all these things existed, they were true,

782
00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:57.470
but yet, still walked away with my best time

783
00:31:57.470 --> 00:31:59.693
and walked away with the victory.

784
00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:02.153
Yeah, and when you first told me the story,

785
00:32:03.075 --> 00:32:05.135
I was so delighting because you had talked

786
00:32:05.135 --> 00:32:07.097
about having all those facts

787
00:32:07.097 --> 00:32:09.000
and the reality of the conditions.

788
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:11.190
And then at that moment you described it

789
00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:13.773
that you just let go.

790
00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:16.530
You dissolved into your greatness

