1049
00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:24.350
And I remember my mom calling me up upstairs

1050
00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:28.390
and she sat down, I'll never forget it.

1051
00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:33.210
We had this orange-like kind of love seat couch

1052
00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:36.160
that we had, she sat down in there, I sat next to her,

1053
00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:38.060
like kinda right between her legs.

1054
00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:42.447
And she said, "Hey, your dad's never coming back."

1055
00:43:44.398 --> 00:43:46.040
And in that moment,

1056
00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:49.340
I decided my first response was, okay,

1057
00:43:49.340 --> 00:43:50.740
I've become the man of the house.

1058
00:43:50.740 --> 00:43:54.630
And so my verbal response was we don't need him.

1059
00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:56.336
We don't need him.

1060
00:43:56.336 --> 00:43:57.990
We'll be fine without him, mom.

1061
00:43:57.990 --> 00:43:59.990
And no, she says, "You don't understand.

1062
00:44:01.249 --> 00:44:02.907
"No, he died.

1063
00:44:02.907 --> 00:44:05.867
"The police found him, and someone shot him."

1064
00:44:07.883 --> 00:44:08.963
And all of a sudden,

1065
00:44:10.970 --> 00:44:12.720
I just froze.

1066
00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:15.180
I didn't even cry, I just froze.

1067
00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:18.033
And I didn't actually cry until actually a year later,

1068
00:44:20.027 --> 00:44:23.320
but I just froze and I didn't know how to respond.

1069
00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:26.520
And then throughout that next year,

1070
00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:29.203
these little mini explosions would happen in my life,

1071
00:44:32.459 --> 00:44:33.950
and my stuttering increased.

1072
00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:36.100
Mini explosions like acting out or.

1073
00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:37.820
Yeah, acting out.
Getting in trouble,

1074
00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:40.203
Getting into trouble, getting into fights,

1075
00:44:42.270 --> 00:44:46.070
just not being a very pleasant kid and all.

1076
00:44:48.918 --> 00:44:51.800
But I just remember that time of being very confused

1077
00:44:51.800 --> 00:44:54.763
and hating my dad because he left us.

1078
00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:59.540
And I carried that ship on my shoulder through high school.

1079
00:44:59.540 --> 00:45:00.940
That's another thing, again,

1080
00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:04.890
the experts thought that I was gonna go down another path,

1081
00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:09.120
you know, finding swimming was a savior for me.

1082
00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:11.140
I mean, how did that happen?

1083
00:45:11.140 --> 00:45:12.560
It saved your life, right.

1084
00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:14.710
Swimming saved my life.

1085
00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:18.240
I credit the blessing and the gift of competitive sports

1086
00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:21.310
and swimming to really changing the angle

1087
00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:24.000
and the pitch of my life.

1088
00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:27.670
Well, one of the things that my mom did right out the bat,

1089
00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:29.310
she just became very devoted

1090
00:45:29.310 --> 00:45:31.940
to getting my sister and I out of the neighborhood

1091
00:45:32.991 --> 00:45:33.824
and used every excuse to do it.

1092
00:45:33.824 --> 00:45:37.343
I remember her kidnapping my friends and I on the Saturdays,

1093
00:45:38.873 --> 00:45:41.855
and we would literally go to the art museum.

1094
00:45:41.855 --> 00:45:42.850
We would go see the Cleveland Orchestra.

1095
00:45:42.850 --> 00:45:44.400
We would do all of these things

1096
00:45:46.478 --> 00:45:49.470
that kids in the hood didn't do.

1097
00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:53.380
And my mom found every excuse to keep us out of the hood.

1098
00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:56.390
And I remember, quick story,

1099
00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:57.870
how I fell into the sport of swimming,

1100
00:45:57.870 --> 00:45:59.820
'cause I literally fell into the sport.

1101
00:46:00.940 --> 00:46:05.940
Gray-Y, Euclid Y, which was a YMCA, that was across town,

1102
00:46:07.820 --> 00:46:10.962
created this program called Gray-Y.

1103
00:46:10.962 --> 00:46:12.650
And it was an initiative to use sports

1104
00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:16.120
to bridge the gap between the white poor kids

1105
00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.580
and the black poor kids who used to always fight.

1106
00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:20.690
And so you would come together in sport

1107
00:46:21.564 --> 00:46:22.440
and that's why they called it Gray-Y.

1108
00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:26.980
And so we started going to this Saturday and Sunday,

1109
00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:29.100
kind of activities in sports.

1110
00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:32.670
And there I was introduced to a lot of different sports.

1111
00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:34.880
The one sport that stuck was swimming,

1112
00:46:34.880 --> 00:46:37.820
but here's the crazy story real quickly.

1113
00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:40.850
My friend and I, Lamar, were playing,

1114
00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:41.950
we were just goofing off.

1115
00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:43.933
I didn't wanna play field hockey,

1116
00:46:44.937 --> 00:46:47.694
and so we decided to just kinda ditch that

1117
00:46:47.694 --> 00:46:50.120
and just hang out in the Y area.

1118
00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:52.140
And we started playing, you know,

1119
00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:53.540
we started hitting each other

1120
00:46:54.516 --> 00:46:55.520
and so he would hit me and start running around

1121
00:46:55.520 --> 00:46:56.520
and I would hit him.

1122
00:46:57.364 --> 00:46:59.810
And so basically, I remember hitting Lamar really hard

1123
00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:02.476
and he gave me that look like if I catch you,

1124
00:47:02.476 --> 00:47:03.657
I'm gonna kill you, kinda look,

1125
00:47:03.657 --> 00:47:04.490
you know, when you play you're kid,

1126
00:47:05.338 --> 00:47:06.370
you hit somebody too hard.

1127
00:47:06.370 --> 00:47:08.770
Well, we started running through the YMCA,

1128
00:47:08.770 --> 00:47:11.453
well, I run onto the pool deck.

1129
00:47:11.453 --> 00:47:13.568
I run and I run on the pool deck

1130
00:47:13.568 --> 00:47:16.800
and literally, almost slipped and fall into the water.

1131
00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:20.890
At the same time, the coach of the Euclid Y Dragonflies

1132
00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:24.100
comes over to me, I think I'm in trouble.

1133
00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:25.760
He starts telling me about how cool

1134
00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:27.810
and how great swimming is.

1135
00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:29.770
And I'm sitting here just wondering

1136
00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:31.240
when the hammer's gonna drop.

1137
00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:32.290
The guy when the kid's gonna show up,

1138
00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:33.687
beat your ass.

1139
00:47:33.687 --> 00:47:34.730
(Byron and Rich laugh out loud)

1140
00:47:34.730 --> 00:47:36.310
And he's talking, he's talking,

1141
00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:39.093
and he's telling me, long story short,

1142
00:47:40.570 --> 00:47:42.173
my mom comes to pick us up.

1143
00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:46.730
Jeff Armstrong, the coach ends up talking to my mom

1144
00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:49.620
about getting me involved in swimming.

1145
00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:51.070
And in that Monday,

1146
00:47:51.070 --> 00:47:54.050
I was on the Euclid Y Dragonflies swim team

1147
00:47:54.050 --> 00:47:55.780
and started swimming.

1148
00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:56.970
And that was at age nine.

1149
00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:59.016
That was when I was nine.

1150
00:47:59.016 --> 00:48:02.600
And I mean, swimming is such an unlikely outlet

1151
00:48:02.600 --> 00:48:05.483
whereas football, whereas basketball, why swimming?

1152
00:48:06.439 --> 00:48:07.620
And how does that happen?

1153
00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:09.910
Were there other black kids on the team?

1154
00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:10.743
No
It must have been

1155
00:48:10.743 --> 00:48:11.640
very unusual.

1156
00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:13.877
I was always a pioneer at the time.

1157
00:48:13.877 --> 00:48:17.180
And I could tell you thousands of stories about (laughs)

1158
00:48:17.180 --> 00:48:19.940
very funny stories about being black

1159
00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:23.811
back in the early eighties, in a competitive sports.

1160
00:48:23.811 --> 00:48:24.644
Yeah, then going to school

1161
00:48:24.644 --> 00:48:26.210
and feel like you do what?

1162
00:48:26.210 --> 00:48:28.110
Yeah, you swim, and believe me,

1163
00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:29.530
I got it from both sides

1164
00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:33.350
from the white kids who didn't see black swimmers

1165
00:48:34.839 --> 00:48:36.989
I became the authority of everything black.

1166
00:48:37.928 --> 00:48:40.543
So, I was the why do black people do this?

1167
00:48:42.094 --> 00:48:43.604
So I would get it from that side.

1168
00:48:43.604 --> 00:48:44.437
Do you know Colin Jones?

1169
00:48:44.437 --> 00:48:46.040
(all laugh out loud)

1170
00:48:46.040 --> 00:48:49.540
And then on the other side with all my black friends,

1171
00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:52.370
at this point, being in competitive swimming,

1172
00:48:52.370 --> 00:48:54.170
I disappeared from the neighborhood.

1173
00:48:55.009 --> 00:48:57.370
And so all my black friends were like,

1174
00:48:57.370 --> 00:48:58.203
where are you?

1175
00:48:58.203 --> 00:48:59.755
What are you doing?

1176
00:48:59.755 --> 00:49:01.405
And they would call me white boy.

1177
00:49:01.405 --> 00:49:03.140
And because at this time I was going through

1178
00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:05.930
to a speech specialist, a therapist,

1179
00:49:05.930 --> 00:49:08.480
because again, I stuttered, and when my dad died,

1180
00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:10.157
it really came out.

1181
00:49:10.157 --> 00:49:11.303
That was like a trauma

1182
00:49:11.303 --> 00:49:12.927
response to that.
A trauma response.

1183
00:49:12.927 --> 00:49:16.370
And also, my mom and the therapist always told me

1184
00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:21.370
to enunciate and really exaggerate as I pronounced words.

1185
00:49:22.573 --> 00:49:23.770
So all my black friends

1186
00:49:24.855 --> 00:49:26.630
and people in the neighborhood called me, white boy.

1187
00:49:26.630 --> 00:49:29.240
And so I had it from both ends,

1188
00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:32.800
but here's the thing, I knew I loved swimming,

1189
00:49:32.800 --> 00:49:36.460
I knew it because that was my creative zone.

1190
00:49:36.460 --> 00:49:39.250
Back then, I didn't know how far it would take me,

1191
00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:42.630
but all I knew at that moment, it was a refuge.

1192
00:49:42.630 --> 00:49:47.170
When I got in the water, I was in my playground,

1193
00:49:47.170 --> 00:49:49.783
I was in the element that just was beautiful to me.

1194
00:49:50.661 --> 00:49:53.660
And I also credit swimming for helping me

1195
00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:55.520
find what my learning style was.

1196
00:49:55.520 --> 00:49:59.750
Back then, in addition to stuttering

1197
00:49:59.750 --> 00:50:02.616
I was misdiagnosed as having ADD

1198
00:50:02.616 --> 00:50:04.363
'cause I couldn't stay still.

1199
00:50:05.485 --> 00:50:06.700
And I'm not the type of learner,

1200
00:50:06.700 --> 00:50:07.533
I mean, we know now

1201
00:50:07.533 --> 00:50:10.298
the whole different modalities of learning,

1202
00:50:10.298 --> 00:50:13.460
we know if you're auditory or kinesthetic or whatever,

1203
00:50:13.460 --> 00:50:17.291
back then I was not, I am to this day,

1204
00:50:17.291 --> 00:50:18.999
I'm not an auditory learner.

1205
00:50:18.999 --> 00:50:20.240
If you ask me to sit in a seat

1206
00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:22.720
and just listen to a professor or a teacher

1207
00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:25.630
for more than 30 minutes, it's done.

1208
00:50:25.630 --> 00:50:26.570
You're not gonna do it.
I'm not gonna do it.

1209
00:50:26.570 --> 00:50:27.983
It's not gonna get in.

1210
00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:33.900
So as a result I was kinda disruptive a lot in school.

1211
00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:37.113
Well, swimming, I recognized, wait a minute.

1212
00:50:38.172 --> 00:50:41.395
If you were to demonstrate to me how to do something

1213
00:50:41.395 --> 00:50:44.120
and then gave me an opportunity to actually do it,

1214
00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:45.690
I could pick it up like that.

1215
00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.190
I could intuitively pick it up.

1216
00:50:48.190 --> 00:50:52.740
And so swimming, I realized I started to excel

1217
00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:54.253
by the first year,

1218
00:50:55.100 --> 00:50:57.936
I was picking up all the different strokes

1219
00:50:57.936 --> 00:51:00.803
and I made it on the Zone Team.

1220
00:51:01.866 --> 00:51:04.610
And first, it was just swimming

1221
00:51:04.610 --> 00:51:07.680
fast enough to swim in different cities

1222
00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:09.250
and then different states.

1223
00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:11.870
And then I was invited to all these different meets

1224
00:51:11.870 --> 00:51:13.465
and I was like, "You know what?

1225
00:51:13.465 --> 00:51:15.025
"I'm gonna stick with swimming

1226
00:51:15.025 --> 00:51:17.100
"because swimming is getting me out of the neighborhood."

1227
00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:18.690
It's expanding your horizons.

1228
00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:20.860
And I'm going to nationals.

1229
00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:22.270
And all these different places.

1230
00:51:22.270 --> 00:51:23.500
And so I'm gonna stick with it.

1231
00:51:23.500 --> 00:51:28.400
And so I credit swimming for kind of finding my ultra

1232
00:51:28.400 --> 00:51:30.330
sort of thing, kinda discovering

1233
00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:34.340
that this is my love zone, my safe haven.

1234
00:51:34.340 --> 00:51:35.590
Well, it's also,

1235
00:51:36.560 --> 00:51:38.050
my story's a lot different from yours,

1236
00:51:38.050 --> 00:51:42.480
but I had certain sort of adolescent pains or whatever,

1237
00:51:42.480 --> 00:51:46.540
and swimming was always my safe place, it was my safe haven.

1238
00:51:46.540 --> 00:51:49.700
And there's something about being underwater

1239
00:51:50.624 --> 00:51:52.090
where none of that can touch you.

1240
00:51:52.090 --> 00:51:53.395
You know what I mean?

1241
00:51:53.395 --> 00:51:54.228
Like, you can't hear anything,

1242
00:51:54.228 --> 00:51:57.300
no one can talk to you and you feel safe.

1243
00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:59.960
it's almost like this womb-like place where you can go

1244
00:51:59.960 --> 00:52:03.840
and escape whatever unpleasant situations

1245
00:52:05.177 --> 00:52:07.690
surround you in your life outside the pool,

1246
00:52:07.690 --> 00:52:09.497
and for you to be able to recognize that

1247
00:52:09.497 --> 00:52:10.763
and lean into it.

1248
00:52:12.402 --> 00:52:13.235
Extraordinary story.
It's amazing, really,

1249
00:52:13.235 --> 00:52:15.370
set you on a completely different trajectory.

1250
00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:16.410
Yeah, it did.

1251
00:52:16.410 --> 00:52:18.220
Do you have friends

1252
00:52:18.220 --> 00:52:20.390
still from those days back there?

1253
00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:22.548
Are they still living back there?

1254
00:52:22.548 --> 00:52:24.680
Or do you keep in touch with any of those guys?

1255
00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:27.480
Well, since my adulthood and since I've been married

1256
00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:30.190
and since my grandmother died

1257
00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:32.450
and my mom moved out to Palm Springs,

1258
00:52:32.450 --> 00:52:37.450
I haven't been back to the neighborhood in over 15 years.

1259
00:52:38.060 --> 00:52:41.810
But I will tell you a story of the last time I was back.

1260
00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:44.040
I remember walking down the street,

1261
00:52:44.040 --> 00:52:48.200
it was in December, it was snow,

1262
00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:53.200
I was smelling just the whole Cleveland vibe is beautiful,

1263
00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:55.460
just kinda reminiscing through the hood.

1264
00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:57.443
All of a sudden I feel this thump,

1265
00:52:58.557 --> 00:52:59.707
this car approaching me

1266
00:53:02.584 --> 00:53:04.246
and music was-
The bass.

1267
00:53:04.246 --> 00:53:06.290
The bass that was happening.

1268
00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:08.400
And then I'm realizing, wait a minute, I'm in the hood.

1269
00:53:08.400 --> 00:53:11.491
And I haven't been here, people don't know who I am.

1270
00:53:11.491 --> 00:53:14.091
So I get a little nervous that this car is creeping.

1271
00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:19.540
I could feel that this car, they were looking at me,

1272
00:53:19.540 --> 00:53:21.719
so the car rolls up.

1273
00:53:21.719 --> 00:53:24.936
And then all of a sudden, the car rolls just right past me.

1274
00:53:24.936 --> 00:53:27.190
And when you grow up in the hood,

1275
00:53:27.190 --> 00:53:30.730
you learn that when you're in the city,

1276
00:53:30.730 --> 00:53:32.540
you don't walk on the sidewalk.

1277
00:53:32.540 --> 00:53:33.960
You never walk on the sidewalk.

1278
00:53:33.960 --> 00:53:35.630
You always walk in the street.

1279
00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:37.590
And the reason why you (laughs) it's like instinct,

1280
00:53:37.590 --> 00:53:39.310
you walk in the street,

1281
00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:41.800
not because you wanna just hold up traffic,

1282
00:53:41.800 --> 00:53:45.310
but that's the best shot of you running

1283
00:53:45.310 --> 00:53:46.680
if something breaks out.

1284
00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:49.580
If you're on the sidewalk, somebody can come and jump you,

1285
00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:54.300
or pull you in a alley or something.

1286
00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:55.860
You just learn to walk in the street.

1287
00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:58.640
So an instinct kicked in, I'm walking in the street,

1288
00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:01.120
all of a sudden, the car pulls up,

1289
00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:02.550
and then it just kinda pulls

1290
00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:04.781
and it stops right in front of me,

1291
00:54:04.781 --> 00:54:08.160
just kinda literally it doesn't go by me.

1292
00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:11.500
It turns, and now I have to purposely

1293
00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:14.980
walk onto the sidewalk to walk around it,

1294
00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:17.990
and so I realized, oh, hold on, so I stop.

1295
00:54:17.990 --> 00:54:20.190
And then the windows roll down

1296
00:54:20.190 --> 00:54:21.430
and I'm thinking I'm in trouble.

1297
00:54:21.430 --> 00:54:23.110
I am in trouble.

1298
00:54:23.110 --> 00:54:26.890
Well, it was (laughs) a good friend of mine.

1299
00:54:26.890 --> 00:54:30.643
We called him Deek, and I won't give his real name,

1300
00:54:31.646 --> 00:54:34.327
but he rolls down and he says,

1301
00:54:34.327 --> 00:54:39.177
"You are crazy, why are you walking in the hood like this?

1302
00:54:39.177 --> 00:54:40.790
"You could get shot."

1303
